# jobhunt

Run the pipeline through the CLI and rate through the digest file or in chat. Never score or
rank listings yourself — `jobhunt score` does that, so results stay reproducible and the
ratings loop stays intact.

## Commands (run from the workspace root)

| Task | Command |
|------|---------|
| Full run: fetch → score → digest | `uv run jobhunt check` (takes minutes: polite 1 req/s scraping) |
| Only re-render the digest | `uv run jobhunt digest` |
| Show what the user rated 4–5 that is still open | `uv run jobhunt shortlist` (also rewrites `shortlist.md`) |
| Everything known about one listing | `uv run jobhunt show <url-or-id>` |
| Add a role from a link | `uv run jobhunt add <url>` (fetches, stores, scores it) |
| Change an earlier rating | edit the `rating:` line in the digest it was rated in, then `uv run jobhunt rate digests/<that-file>.md` |
| Re-score everything after the user edited `profile/profile.md` or `profile/preferences.md` | `uv run jobhunt check --rescore` (replaces old scores; one Claude call per batch of 10, so mention it takes a while) |
| Ingest ratings from the newest digest | `uv run jobhunt rate` (add `--force` to regenerate preferences with < 3 new ratings) |
| Regenerate the learned preferences on their own | `uv run jobhunt learn` |
| Check whether the scores match how the user actually rates | `uv run jobhunt calibration` (rank correlation + mean rating per band; needs ~10 ratings to mean anything) |
| Show the listings the scoring got most wrong | the **Calibration** page in `uv run jobhunt serve` — notes added there are read by the next `learn` |
| Newest digest file | highest `digests/YYYY-MM-DD[-N].md` by date, then N (`-2` sorts *before* `.md` — don't trust `ls | tail`) |
| Browser UI (buttons, rating queue, editors, settings) | `uv run jobhunt serve` — runs until Ctrl-C, so start it in a terminal the user controls, not from a tool call that waits for exit |
| Why no update banner appeared | `uv run jobhunt update --check` |

Settings (theme, score range, digest size, what the Rated page hides, update checks) live in
`config/settings.yaml` and on the UI's Settings page. Adding a *site* is a different job:
that is `config/sources.yaml` and the `jobhunt-sources` skill.

## Presenting a digest

Read the newest digest. If it has a `## Shortlist` section (listings the user already rated
4–5 that are still open, nearest deadline first), show it first, briefly. Then show the top N
(default 10) of the numbered queue as a list, one line each:
`N. Title — Employer · score S · role · deadline D · [link]`, then the digest's **Why** line.
Mention the header's `Source errors` / `Manual:` links if present. Skip `## Unscored` entries
unless asked. Shortlist lines have no `rating:` field — they are not for rating again.

## Talking about roles in chat

When the user wants to think out loud about a role rather than rate it:

1. Read `profile/profile.md`, `profile/preferences.md` and `docs/cv.md` first — the answer has
   to be about *this* person, not about the role in general. Say which constraint or
   preference you are leaning on.
2. Get the engine's own verdict with `uv run jobhunt show <url-or-id>`: its score, the one-line
   **Why**, the **Concerns**, and the rating if they already gave one. Use that as the starting
   point and disagree with it explicitly if you do — don't silently substitute your own score.
3. Answer the question they asked (is this worth applying to, what would the application need,
   what is the weakest part of the fit). Name the specific overlap with their CV, and the
   specific risk, rather than a general verdict.
4. If they reach a view worth keeping — "I would take a teaching load for the right lab" —
   offer to write it into `## Manual` in `profile/preferences.md` (see below). Rating it in the
   queue is the other durable option; both beat leaving it in the chat.

## A link the user pastes

`uv run jobhunt add <url>` fetches the page, stores it under the `manual` source and scores it
with one Claude call, so it joins the queue, the digest and the ratings loop like a fetched
listing. Report the score and the **Why** it prints.

If the command reports that the site returned 403 or a sign-in wall, **do not** try to work
around it. Ask the user to open the page and paste the text, then:
`uv run jobhunt add <url> --title "…" --employer "…" --description "…"`.

For a whole site the user wants watched from now on, use the `jobhunt-sources` skill instead.

## Recording ratings the user gives in chat

Each digest entry has `<!-- id: … -->`, `rating:` and `note:` lines. For each rating:
1. Find the entry by its number heading (`## 3. …`), fill `rating: <1-5>` and `note: <text>`
   (leave `note:` empty if none). Edit those two lines only.
2. Run `uv run jobhunt rate`. Report the `N rating(s) ingested` line and the `preferences:` line.

Scale: 5 apply · 4 strong · 3 maybe · 2 not really · 1 irrelevant.

## Re-aligning the preferences the engine inferred

`## Learned` and `## Specifics` in `profile/preferences.md` are written by the engine from the
ratings. They are a guess, and the user may disagree with one. **Never edit those two sections
yourself** — the next `jobhunt rate` / `jobhunt learn` regenerates them and your edit is gone.

The section that outranks them is `## Manual`: the user's own rules, which the engine is told
never to contradict. So a disagreement is resolved by writing a Manual rule:

1. Read the file and quote the rule they are reacting to, verbatim.
2. Ask what is actually true instead — one or two questions, not an interview. "Never
   Rotterdam" might mean "not Rotterdam for a 4-year PhD" or "not unless it is this one lab".
3. Draft one sentence in their words, as specific as their answer was, and show it to them
   before writing anything.
4. On their yes, add it under `## Manual` (keep the existing bullets; edit one in place if it
   is the same rule refined) and leave every other section untouched.
5. Run `uv run jobhunt learn` so the generated sections are rewritten with the new Manual rule
   as fixed context, and report what changed in `## Learned` / `## Specifics`.
6. If scores should reflect it now, offer `uv run jobhunt check --rescore` and say that it
   costs one Claude call per 10 open listings.

A rule they wrote is also the right home for something the ratings cannot express: "I will
move for a lab I care about, but not for a job I merely like."

## Known failure modes

| Output | Meaning / what to do |
|--------|----------------------|
| `preferences: failed` or scoring errors mentioning `OAuth session expired` / `authenticate` | The shell `claude` CLI is logged out. Ratings are still saved. Tell the user to run `claude login` in a terminal, then `uv run jobhunt learn` (or `uv run jobhunt score`). Don't call `claude -p` yourself to diagnose. |
| `preferences: skipped (fewer than 3 new ratings…)` | Normal. Nothing to do unless the user wants `--force`. |
| `Source errors: … 429` / `502` | Transient site rate-limits; the run continued. Mention it, don't retry. |
| `Manual: [Indeed: …]` links | Indeed blocks scrapers; these are saved searches for the user to open. |
| `… returned 403 …` from `jobhunt add` | The site refuses scrapers. Ask for the text and pass `--title/--employer/--description`; never work around it. |
| `no update check: …` | The engine cannot check GitHub for updates. The message says why (a development checkout, a commit pin, or an install that did not come from git). |
